<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::security_incident_freque" target="ai_data_backlash::political_investigations" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">A higher frequency of strikes and sabotage attributed to foreign actors creates political pressure that leads parliaments and prosecutors to open inquiries into procurement, export controls, and tech firms' foreign ties, increasing investigative activity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_data_backlash</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::security_incident_freque" target="us_china_strategic_rival::regional_conflict_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">An increased tempo and normalization of kinetic incidents in Ukraine provides operational lessons and legitimizes harder military postures that accelerate force posture adjustments and riskier maneuvers in the Indo‑Pacific, raising regional conflict intensity.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::security_incident_freque" target="us_political_realign::executive_autonomy" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">A rising tempo of security incidents tied to the Russia–Ukraine war enables the U.S. executive to invoke national-security authorities and emergency justifications for sanctions, deployments, and intelligence actions, producing faster unilateral decision-making and greater deference from Congress and courts.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_political_realign</data>
</edge>
