  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A visible increase in regulatory scrutiny of tech/data practices triggers legislators to launch focused hearings and investigations into foreign transfers and national-security implications, directly increasing oversight activity related to Iran–Gulf interactions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_data_backlash</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_gulf_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ai_data_backlash::regulatory_scrutiny" target="russia_ukraine_war::supply_chain_disruption_" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">New and amplified regulatory requirements (export controls, customs inspections, compliance checks) on AI-related hardware/software raise paperwork, require supplier relabeling and vendor audits, and trigger rapid supplier switching that causes immediate delays and bottlenecks in shipments of semiconductors, sensors and drone components used in the Russia–Ukraine theater.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_data_backlash</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ai_data_backlash::regulatory_scrutiny" target="us_china_strategic_rival::policy_uncertainty" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A rapid rise in regulatory scrutiny of AI and data practices creates ambiguous compliance obligations and signals likely new cross‑border restrictions, directly increasing near‑term unpredictability about US policy and regulatory responses toward China.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_data_backlash</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ai_data_backlash::regulatory_scrutiny" target="us_political_realign::regulatory_uncertainty" id="0">
